cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
title: "sctrap: trap frequencies of magnetically levitated particles over arbitrary superconductor geometries"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Francis
    family-names: Headley
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-7585-4957"
    affiliation: "Institut für Theoretische Physik, Eberhard Karls Universität Tübingen, Tübingen, Germany"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-06-11
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/F-Heidemann/sctrap"
url: "https://github.com/F-Heidemann/sctrap"
# TODO: after you cut a tagged release and link the repo to Zenodo, paste the
# minted DOI here (and add `identifiers:` if you want the versioned DOI too).
# doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
keywords:
  - superconductor
  - magnetic levitation
  - magnetomechanics
  - trap frequencies
  - finite element method
  - scikit-fem
  - gmsh
abstract: >-
  sctrap computes the equilibrium position, levitation height and the five
  trap frequencies (three translational, two librational) of a magnetic dipole
  levitated above an arbitrary superconductor geometry, directly from a gmsh
  mesh. It solves the magnetic-scalar-potential Laplace problem on the air
  domain with a Meissner (Neumann) condition on the superconductor surface
  using scikit-fem, in pure Python with no conda, MPI or PETSc dependency.
  Results are validated against analytic image-dipole limits and against
  published levitation experiments.
